const fdeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · fetch', note: 'The control unit (CU) sends the address of the next instruction along the address bus, and the instruction is fetched from internal memory (RAM) into the CPU. Programs are held in RAM precisely so the CPU can fetch from them at full speed.' },
    { math: '2 · decode', note: 'Inside the control unit the instruction is decoded: what operation is this — add, compare, copy, jump — and what data does it act on? The CU directs the rest of the CPU accordingly; it is the traffic controller of the system.' },
    { math: '3 · execute', note: 'The arithmetic and logic unit (ALU) does the work: addition, comparison, logical operations. The result is written back, the program counter moves on, and the cycle starts again — billions of times per second, which is what a clock speed measured in GHz means.' },
    { math: '4 · RAM — the volatile workspace', note: 'Random access memory is read/write and volatile: it holds the operating system, programs and data currently in use, and loses everything at power-off. Backing storage (a hard drive or SSD) is slower but keeps its contents — which is why work must be saved.' },
    { math: '5 · ROM — the bootstrap', note: 'Read-only memory is non-volatile and (as its name says) read-only in normal use: its startup instructions are fixed at manufacture. It is what the CPU executes the instant power arrives, waking the machine far enough to load the operating system from storage into RAM.' },
  ],
