<TopicPage slug="subjects/information-technology/foundational/types-and-components-of-computer-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every computer system — from a desktop under a desk to the smartphone in a pocket — is one
    idea repeated: <strong>hardware</strong>, the physical components, running
    <strong>software</strong>, the programs that control the operation of the computer and
    process the data. Cambridge IGCSE ICT 0417 opens with exactly this distinction (section 1.1)
    and then dissects the machine: the CPU that processes instructions (1.2), the operating
    system that lets you talk to it (1.3), the types of computer those parts get packed into
    (1.4), and the emerging technologies — artificial intelligence and extended reality — that
    are redrawing the category (1.5).
  </p>

  <h2>Inside the machine: fetch, decode, execute</h2>
  <p>
    The CPU's job, in the syllabus's words, is processing instructions entered into the computer
    in order to produce an output. It does this with a three-beat loop — fetch, decode, execute —
    shared between its two headline parts: the control unit that sequences and directs, and the
    arithmetic and logic unit that computes. The loop's memory partners matter as much as the
    loop itself, and the RAM/ROM split is a guaranteed exam question. Walk the whole cycle.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the fetch–decode–execute cycle, plus the two memories that make it possible — RAM and ROM" widget="equationStep" config={fdeCfg} />

  <p>
    The memory split is worth stating as a contrast table you can recite: <strong>RAM</strong> —
    read/write, volatile, holds what is currently in use, contents lost on power-off;
    <strong>ROM</strong> — read-only, non-volatile, holds the bootstrap, contents fixed at
    manufacture. One is a desk you work on; the other is the instructions glued to the inside of
    the machine's lid telling it how to wake up. Everything else the syllabus lists as hardware
    — motherboard, graphics card, sound card, network interface card, storage devices, input and
    output devices — exists to feed the cycle or carry its results.
  </p>

  <h2>The software half</h2>
  <p>
    Software divides into two families. <strong>Applications software</strong> provides the
    services the <em>user</em> requires to solve a task — word processing, spreadsheets,
    databases, video and graphics editing, CAD. <strong>System software</strong> provides the
    services the <em>computer</em> requires to operate — operating systems, device drivers,
    utilities, compilers and linkers. The operating system itself is the bridge: through a
    command line interface (CLI) you type the instructions; through a graphical user interface
    (GUI) you point and click; dialogue-based and gesture-based interfaces (talk to it, swipe
    it) now cover everything from smart speakers to tablets. Same machine, different contract
    with the human.
  </p>

  <h2>Why your phone exists: one line on a log plot</h2>
  <p>
    The types of computer in syllabus 1.4 — desktop, laptop, tablet, smartphone, phablet — are
    not really different machines; they are the same machine at different points of a
    decades-long shrink. The plot below shows the number of transistors on a flagship CPU on a
    log<sub>10</sub> scale: each gridline is ten times more transistors. Intel's 4004 of 1971
    carried about 2,300 (log<sub>10</sub> ≈ 3.36); the 2022 Apple M1 Ultra carries about 114
    billion (log<sub>10</sub> ≈ 11.06). A roughly straight line on a log scale is exponential
    growth — Moore's law — and it is the reason a 1970s computer filled a room that today fits
    in a phone, trading the desktop's expandability for the mobile computer's portability.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="transistor counts of flagship CPUs, 1971–2022, on a log10 scale — a straight line means exponential growth" widget="plotter" config={mooreCfg} />

  <h2>What the shrink enables</h2>
  <p>
    Section 1.5 asks about the impact of emerging technologies, and two headline it: artificial
    intelligence, where the transistor budget buys learning rather than just calculation, and
    extended reality — virtual and augmented — where portable processors, sensors and displays
    overlay digital content on the world. Both are consequences of the line in the graph above:
    more computation, less power, smaller packages. The syllabus's request that you discuss
    their impact on everyday life is really a question about that curve continuing.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE ICT 0417 syllabus 2026–2028, section 1 "Types and components of computer systems" (1.1–1.5); Intel, processor specifications (4004, 386, Pentium, Pentium 4, Core i7); Apple, M1 and M1 Ultra technical specifications (2020–2022); G. Moore, <em>Cramming more components onto integrated circuits</em>, Electronics (1965).</p>
</TopicPage>
